 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Rural exodus is the movement of people, mostly young, from villages to towns.\nCauses (push factors in villages): few jobs and low farm incomes, lack of electricity, water, schools and hospitals, hard farm work, land shortage, conflicts and insecurity.\nCauses (pull factors in towns): jobs, higher wages, schools and universities, hospitals, entertainment, modern life.\nConsequences: in villages — fewer young workers, ageing population, less food produced, abandoned farms; in towns — overcrowding, unemployment, shanty towns, crime.\nMaking the countryside more attractive: rural electrification and water, good roads, schools and health centres, credit and modern tools for farmers, agro-industries and cooperatives, youth farming projects, internet access.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Give one push factor and one pull factor of rural exodus.",
  "steps": [
   "A push factor drives people out of the village: lack of jobs.",
   "A pull factor attracts them to the town: higher wages."
  ],
  "answer": "Push: lack of jobs in the village; pull: higher wages in town.",
  "checks": []
 },
